WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:16.520
Here's a question that seems pretty basic
and pretty simple, but it's really not quite

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.210
so straightforward.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:22.080
How does an economic recession or depression
end?

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.270
In other words, we know that they happen.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:27.130
And we know that eventually they end.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:29.080
But what is the mechanism exactly?

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:30.810
We might be in a recession right now.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:32.630
We could be heading into a depression right
now.

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:34.430
This could extend for months or years.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:39.000
Some are saying to some degree we are going
to have very high unemployment for several,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.060
several years at some point.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:45.290
What we are in will end unless this is the
end of planet Earth.

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.140
How does it all end?

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:53.580
We know that we have a lot of self-fulfilling
prophecies that can get us into a recession.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:58.030
But can those same self-fulfilling prophecies
get us out now?

00:00:58.030 --> 00:00:59.269
This is not.

00:00:59.269 --> 00:01:00.269
We know.

00:01:00.269 --> 00:01:01.269
When do we.

00:01:01.269 --> 00:01:04.120
When we say that something has ended.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:09.620
This is not about the statistic that tells
us we are out of the recession or the depression.

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:10.910
We can argue about that.

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.750
And some people would say, well, it's based
on GDP.

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.670
GDP tells you when you're out of a recession.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:20.810
What I'm talking about is not how can we tell
when we're out?

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:28.280
I'm talking about what is the mechanism that
gets an economy out of a recession or a depression?

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.450
Is it that we see more hiring and unemployment
goes down?

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:35.290
Is it that the stock market goes up and makes
people confident?

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:40.960
Is it just people's confidence in and of itself,
which is a cause versus an effect?

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.969
These are not simple economic questions.

00:01:43.969 --> 00:01:49.930
And what you really need are two major things
to get out of an economic downturn.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:54.700
First, the cause of the downturn needs to
be clearly resolved.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:02:00.170
And the number two, you need a combination
of economic policy and business conditions

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:03.950
to start an actual recovery in terms of the
numbers.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.600
But you also need consumer sentiment to change.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:12.500
This is a reality in economies like ours that
are heavily driven by the perception of their

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.350
own state.

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:21.080
And that is that people fearing a recession
starting to spend less can precipitate one.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:25.379
But also people feeling as though the situation
has been resolved and starting to spend more

00:02:25.379 --> 00:02:29.970
and demand more in and of itself can help
to get us out of a recession.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:37.400
So, yeah, people feeling like the recession
is over is a big part of the recession being

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:41.100
over and it can seem recursive or endlessly
regressive.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.080
This is arguably not good about our economic
system.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:50.730
I think it's important to understand that
so much of how we are doing as an economy

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:54.750
is about how people believe that we are doing.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.260
But for now, this is our system.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:01.450
And consumer sentiment is part of getting
out of a depression, because when people believe

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:07.150
we are out or soon to be out, they start acting
as though we are out.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:10.739
Which gets us out because people start spending
and demanding goods and services.

00:03:10.739 --> 00:03:13.280
So let's think back to 2008, OK?

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:15.840
The Great Recession of 2008.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.920
Two things happened to stop that recession
and to get us out of it.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:27.349
Number one, the underlying issue, which was
the fear of huge financial institutions being

00:03:27.349 --> 00:03:33.590
insolvent as a result of the mortgage crisis
and some other reasons that was resolved,

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:36.879
sort of in the sense that we had some new
regulations.

00:03:36.879 --> 00:03:42.580
We had the belief that we've put in place
new lending guidelines so the mortgage crisis

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:43.670
can't happen again.

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:47.810
We've cleaned up some corruption, supposedly,
even though nobody went to jail.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:52.780
But the point is there was the belief that
the underlying cause had been resolved.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:54.390
That was a big part of it.

00:03:54.390 --> 00:04:00.569
In addition, though, we had a lot of economic
policy response under the Obama administration

00:04:00.569 --> 00:04:01.569
that got us out.

00:04:01.569 --> 00:04:06.560
That included that huge stimulus package that
Republicans were furious about, that Barack

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:07.560
Obama did.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.159
It included new government spending.

00:04:09.159 --> 00:04:11.220
It included some tax cuts.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:13.739
So that was 2008.

00:04:13.739 --> 00:04:18.930
The difference now with the current recession
or what some are saying is an inevitable depression

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:20.230
that's coming.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:23.980
Related to coronavirus is that it is not a
financial institution.

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:24.980
Recession in principle.

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:30.770
What we have here is a corona virus pandemic,
which decimated demand for stuff because of

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:36.610
the pandemic, because of the virus, demand
for travel went down 90 95 percent.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:40.889
In some cases, demand for office space collapsed.

00:04:40.889 --> 00:04:42.850
Demand for restaurants collapsed.

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:44.300
All of that stuff.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:49.570
So the resolution to this one is not about
government spending money or sending people

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:50.570
cash.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:56.009
Government needs to do that as a stopgap so
that people don't starve or lose their homes

00:04:56.009 --> 00:04:59.770
or lose their cars or lose their businesses
that they hope to go back to.

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:03.280
But the underlying issue here to be solved
is a medical issue.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:04.280
We need treatment.

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:05.830
We need a vaccine.

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:11.039
So for now, government's role is a little
bit different in this recession than it was

00:05:11.039 --> 00:05:12.039
in 2008.

00:05:12.039 --> 00:05:17.630
Government needs to provide support for people
to keep them and businesses afloat in a sort

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:18.770
of waiting period.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:20.580
It's a bridge in a sense.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:26.380
Once the underlying issue, the virus is resolved,
demand in principle should start to recover.

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:31.010
Now it's only going to recover in proportion
to people's ability to demand things.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:33.860
Now, when we say demand, we're talking about
it economically.

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:38.840
I can demand a private jet in the sense that
it sounds cool.

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:44.240
But when I talk about economic demand, it
means that there is actually the money with

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.800
which to demand these things in an economic
system.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.880
So as people feel safe going to restaurants,
they will go to restaurants and the restaurants

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:55.360
will make money.

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:56.759
They'll pay employees.

00:05:56.759 --> 00:05:59.220
They'll buy stuff from food supply companies.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:02.940
The employees will go out and spend money
at other local businesses.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:06.600
The food supply companies seeing more demand
from restaurants will start ordering more

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:08.729
food from producers.

00:06:08.729 --> 00:06:11.349
And the economic activity will resume.

00:06:11.349 --> 00:06:16.480
Traveling will, you know, then increase due
to increased air travel will mean that people

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:20.259
are going to be places they don't live, which
means they will demand hotel stays.

00:06:20.259 --> 00:06:21.270
And on and on and on.

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:23.830
Now, a reasonable question would be.

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:29.480
And one that we should be asking, is there
a way to prevent these recessions in the first

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:30.480
place?

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:32.259
Now, preventing them altogether is tough.

00:06:32.259 --> 00:06:37.510
And there's a difference between a medically
based recession like corona virus and something

00:06:37.510 --> 00:06:40.660
like we saw in 2008, the financial crisis.

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:47.039
There is no doubt that we could organize economies
in a way that would soften these recessions

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:48.860
and make them less severe.

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:52.789
There are some realities about our economic
system.

00:06:52.789 --> 00:06:54.169
There are booms and busts.

00:06:54.169 --> 00:06:55.740
That's how capitalism works.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:07:01.160
And we have a capitalism in the United States
that is not nearly regulated enough.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:05.370
There is no incentive in systems like ours
to be proactive.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:08.470
Proactive means you're spending money you
might not need to spend and you're reducing

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:09.789
your profits in the immediate.

00:07:09.789 --> 00:07:14.160
That that we being proactive is not incentivized
by the system that we have.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:15.850
We are reactive.

00:07:15.850 --> 00:07:22.480
So when the boom turns to a bust, it's really
big and a ton of people suffer greatly in

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:23.870
the last three months.

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:30.699
We learned about this many systems that feel
strong, low unemployment, high stock market,

00:07:30.699 --> 00:07:34.660
high consumer confidence looked like a very
strong infrastructure.

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:37.980
It was really quite fragile in about a two
week period.

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:39.340
It imploded.

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:46.120
If all along we had a more solid infrastructure,
we would suffer not nearly as much during

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:47.120
these downturns.

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:51.889
So, for example, if everybody had health care
not connected to their employer, losing a

00:07:51.889 --> 00:07:55.449
job during a pandemic would be less of a crisis.

00:07:55.449 --> 00:07:59.789
It would still be a crisis, but it would be
less of a crisis if health insurance was not

00:07:59.789 --> 00:08:01.199
tied to employment.

00:08:01.199 --> 00:08:07.680
Another example, if we had universal basic
income or something like it, jobs guarantee

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:15.139
whatever flavor you like, the loss of jobs
or reductions in wages would be less problematic.

00:08:15.139 --> 00:08:19.349
It wouldn't be irrelevant, but it would be
less urgently problematic.

00:08:19.349 --> 00:08:22.820
And the government would not be in as much
of a position of acutely having to figure

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:23.820
out.

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:26.751
We've got to start sending out checks, direct
deposits, debit, prepaid debit cards, all

00:08:26.751 --> 00:08:27.840
this different stuff.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:29.660
It would be less of a crisis.

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:36.870
So the lesson that hopefully we would learn
here is we can't prevent a future pandemic

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:39.570
from a novel virus could happen again.

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:41.240
It likely will happen again.

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:50.200
We can't prevent any up and down in the economy,
even if we ensure by putting in different

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:51.210
insurance policies.

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:54.980
There are business cycles and there are going
to be ups and downs.

00:08:54.980 --> 00:09:00.820
But with Social Democrats like me, want is
more investment from the fruits of when things

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:06.220
are going well to put in place social insurance
policies that will raise the standard of living

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:07.670
for everybody.

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:12.860
Get more people off of that borderline paycheck
to paycheck situation where if you don't work

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:17.970
for two weeks, you now are unable to meet
your basic expenses.

00:09:17.970 --> 00:10:11.870
Let's make the system less fragile

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:42.560
for 
when things like this happen in the future

